  <reporting-org ref="47045" secondary-reporter="0" type="30">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Global Fund to Fight AIDS, Tuberculosis and Malaria</narrative>
  </reporting-org>
  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Madagascar: from malaria control to elimination 2007-2012 - Madagascar (01-Oct-2010-30-Jun-2014)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Malaria is a major public health issue in Madagascar with 92 percent of the population exposed to risk. The overall goal of the program supported by this grant is to contribute to stopping malaria transmission in the central highlands region and to significantly reduce malaria transmission in endemic areas by 2012.  Grant funds are being used to manage malaria cases in appropriate ways at health facilities and at the community level; implement an indoor residual spraying campaign in epidemic risk zones; ensure the availability of management tools related to distribution of long-lasting insecticide-treated bed nets; train health service providers in endemic zones and in the border zones of the central highlands on focused prenatal care and intermittent preventive treatment; and to implement epidemiological  surveillance through sentinel sits in the highlands, the borders and on the west coast of the country.  The program targets high-risk population groups, pregnant women and children under age five.</narrative>
  </description>
